{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts inherits the surrounding application instead of installing a\nglobal visual theme. The default chart theme uses:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"currentColor"},{"type":"text","value":" for foreground, muted text, and grids;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transparent"},{"type":"text","value":" for the chart background;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"six CSS-variable-backed categorical colors."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set the container's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" and the chart follows normal light and dark CSS:"}]},{"type":"code","value":".chart-card {\n  color: #172033;\n  background: #ffffff;\n}\n\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\n  .chart-card {\n    color: #e6edf7;\n    background: #111827;\n  }\n}","lang":"css"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"palette-tokens","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Palette tokens"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Override the default categorical palette at any container boundary:"}]},{"type":"code","value":".revenue-chart {\n  --ts-chart-1: #2563eb;\n  --ts-chart-2: #f97316;\n  --ts-chart-3: #10b981;\n  --ts-chart-4: #8b5cf6;\n  --ts-chart-5: #ec4899;\n  --ts-chart-6: #06b6d4;\n}","lang":"css"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the lowest-cost path for application branding. It also preserves\nautomatic theme changes without rebuilding a chart definition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"definition-level-theme","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definition-level theme"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"theme"},{"type":"text","value":" when a chart needs explicit scene colors:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: x,\n    y: y,\n  },\n\n  theme: {\n    foreground: '#e5e7eb',\n    muted: '#94a3b8',\n    grid: '#334155',\n    background: '#0f172a',\n    palette: ['#38bdf8', '#fb7185', '#4ade80'],\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"theme"},{"type":"text","value":" is partial. Omitted fields retain defaults. A responsive definition's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chart"},{"type":"text","value":" context receives the default build-time theme, which is useful when\nmarks need the shared palette or foreground tokens. A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"theme"},{"type":"text","value":" returned by that\nsame builder is merged afterward while the scene is created, so read an\napplication-supplied theme from the builder's captured values when it must use\nthose overrides."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not encode semantic status by reading the current theme in data\npreparation. Keep meaning stable and choose theme-appropriate paint at render\ntime."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"mark-styling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark styling"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in marks expose the paint styles relevant to their geometry: fill, stroke,\nopacity, widths, line caps, dashes, corner radius, and font properties. A style\ncan be fixed or data-driven where the mark's option accepts a visual channel."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep these responsibilities separate:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"scales map semantic values to visual values;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"mark options select and refine paint;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the theme supplies shared defaults;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"application CSS controls the surrounding surface."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For categorical or quantitative color mapping, use the canonical\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./legends-and-color.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legends and Color"}]},{"type":"text","value":" guide."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"axis-title-styling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis title styling"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"String axis titles use the chart foreground and built-in title typography.\nUse the object form when one title needs an explicit text color, opacity, size,\nweight, or offset:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale, axis: { label: 'Month' } },\n    y: {\n      scale: yScale,\n      axis: {\n        label: {\n          text: 'Revenue (USD)',\n          fontSize: 14,\n          fontWeight: 700,\n          fill: '#2563eb',\n          opacity: 0.85,\n        },\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fill"},{"type":"text","value":" is the title's text color. The layout measures the configured font size\nand weight before resolving automatic guide margins, so larger titles do not\nneed matching manual margins."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"canvas-styling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Canvas styling"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Canvas renderer resolves scene paints such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"currentColor"},{"type":"text","value":" and CSS\ncustom properties against the chart's computed environment. It inherits the\nroot font and repaints after relevant ancestor class, style, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data-theme"},{"type":"text","value":",\ncolor-scheme, forced-colors, or viewport changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rasterized scene nodes are not DOM descendants. A node's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"},{"type":"text","value":" therefore\ncannot be targeted by a CSS selector after paint. Put data-dependent fill,\nstroke, opacity, and font choices in mark options or the chart theme; use\ncontainer CSS for palette variables, inherited color, and typography."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"gradients-and-clipping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gradients and clipping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gradients are opt-in resources. Declare them on the chart:"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"gradient-area-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"gradient-area","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { areaY, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\\nimport { rows } from './data'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    areaY(rows, {\\n      x: 'month',\\n      y: 'revenue',\\n      fill: 'url(#revenue-fill)',\\n    }),\\n    lineY(rows, {\\n      x: 'month',\\n      y: 'revenue',\\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\\n      strokeWidth: 2,\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: { scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2) },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Revenue (USD)' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n\\n  gradients: [\\n    {\\n      id: 'revenue-fill',\\n      x1: 0,\\n      y1: 1,\\n      x2: 0,\\n      y2: 0,\\n      stops: [\\n        { offset: 0, color: '#2563eb', opacity: 0.08 },\\n        { offset: 1, color: '#2563eb', opacity: 0.7 },\\n      ],\\n    },\\n  ],\\n  clip: true,\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export const rows = [\\n  { month: 'Jan', revenue: 36_000 },\\n  { month: 'Feb', revenue: 48_000 },\\n  { month: 'Mar', revenue: 45_000 },\\n  { month: 'Apr', revenue: 62_000 },\\n  { month: 'May', revenue: 76_000 },\\n  { month: 'Jun', revenue: 71_000 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { areaY, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    areaY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'revenue',\n      fill: 'url(#revenue-fill)',\n    }),\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'revenue',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 2,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2) },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Revenue (USD)' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  gradients: [\n    {\n      id: 'revenue-fill',\n      x1: 0,\n      y1: 1,\n      x2: 0,\n      y2: 0,\n      stops: [\n        { offset: 0, color: '#2563eb', opacity: 0.08 },\n        { offset: 1, color: '#2563eb', opacity: 0.7 },\n      ],\n    },\n  ],\n  clip: true,\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=gradient-area env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export const rows = [\n  { month: 'Jan', revenue: 36_000 },\n  { month: 'Feb', revenue: 48_000 },\n  { month: 'Mar', revenue: 45_000 },\n  { month: 'Apr', revenue: 62_000 },\n  { month: 'May', revenue: 76_000 },\n  { month: 'Jun', revenue: 71_000 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=gradient-area file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"url(#revenue-fill)"},{"type":"text","value":" as the mark paint. Default SVG hosts emit and scope the\nresource; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"idPrefix"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps resource and clip IDs distinct when several charts\nshare a document."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clip: true"},{"type":"text","value":" when marks should be clipped to the resolved plot rectangle.\nClipping is a geometry policy, not a substitute for correct scale domains."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG and React Native consume both linear and radial gradients for fills and\nstrokes. Canvas consumes linear fills and strokes plus radial fills. It maps a\nradial fill through each shape's normalized bounds, so a non-square shape has\nthe same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"objectBoundingBox"},{"type":"text","value":" ellipse as SVG, then clips the paint to the shape.\nA Canvas gradient needs measurable node bounds; path-only geometry with no\npoint bounds should use an explicit paint instead. Canvas rejects radial\nstrokes because the required nonuniform transform would distort stroke width."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"html-tooltip-styling","children":[{"type":"text","value":"HTML tooltip styling"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The built-in DOM tooltip is an HTML element inside the chart container by default.\nGive it a class through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tooltip.className"},{"type":"text","value":" and style that class in application\nCSS:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\n\nconst definition = defineChart(baseDefinition, {\n  tooltip: { use: tooltip, className: 'revenue-tooltip' },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default tooltip chrome also reads CSS variables from the chart container.\nThis avoids selector specificity fights with its positioning styles:"}]},{"type":"code","value":".revenue-chart {\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-background: color-mix(in srgb, Canvas 92%, transparent);\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-color: CanvasText;\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-border: 1px solid\n    color-mix(in srgb, CanvasText 12%, transparent);\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-border-radius: 0.625rem;\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-shadow: 0 12px 34px\n    color-mix(in srgb, CanvasText 14%, transparent);\n}","lang":"css"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-max-width"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-padding"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"--ts-chart-tooltip-font"},{"type":"text","value":" control the remaining surface defaults. A\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"},{"type":"text","value":" is still useful for content-specific layout."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"portal"},{"type":"text","value":" extension, the preferred manual-Popover path keeps the\nelement under the chart in the DOM, so inheritance and scoped selectors\ncontinue to work. If Popover is unavailable or fails, the fixed fallback moves\nthe element under the chart's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ownerDocument"},{"type":"text","value":" body. Use a document-level\nselector for its class and put required fallback tokens on that class or a\nshared document ancestor."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every framework adapter can compose native application content with the\ndefault rows. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/themes-and-motion.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Themes and Motion examples"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for complete\ncards with inherited palettes, gradients, controls, and optional motion."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"theme-checklist","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Theme checklist"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default charts inherit text color and respond to the application's color\nscheme."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Palette CSS variables are scoped to the smallest useful container."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Semantic status remains distinguishable without color alone."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit chart themes meet contrast requirements in every supported mode."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resource IDs use a stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"idPrefix"},{"type":"text","value":" when charts share a document."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A theme change does not recreate application data or lose focused state."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"palette-tokens","text":"Palette tokens","level":2},{"id":"definition-level-theme","text":"Definition-level theme","level":2},{"id":"mark-styling","text":"Mark styling","level":2},{"id":"axis-title-styling","text":"Axis title styling","level":2},{"id":"canvas-styling","text":"Canvas styling","level":2},{"id":"gradients-and-clipping","text":"Gradients and clipping","level":2},{"id":"html-tooltip-styling","text":"HTML tooltip styling","level":2},{"id":"theme-checklist","text":"Theme checklist","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Charts inherits the surrounding application instead of installing a\nglobal visual theme. The default chart theme uses:\n\n- `currentColor` for foreground, muted text, and grids;\n- `transparent` for the chart background;\n- six CSS-variable-backed categorical colors.\n\nSet the container's `color` and the chart follows normal light and dark CSS:\n\n```css\n.chart-card {\n  color: #172033;\n  background: #ffffff;\n}\n\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\n  .chart-card {\n    color: #e6edf7;\n    background: #111827;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Palette tokens\n\nOverride the default categorical palette at any container boundary:\n\n```css\n.revenue-chart {\n  --ts-chart-1: #2563eb;\n  --ts-chart-2: #f97316;\n  --ts-chart-3: #10b981;\n  --ts-chart-4: #8b5cf6;\n  --ts-chart-5: #ec4899;\n  --ts-chart-6: #06b6d4;\n}\n```\n\nThis is the lowest-cost path for application branding. It also preserves\nautomatic theme changes without rebuilding a chart definition.\n\n## Definition-level theme\n\nUse `theme` when a chart needs explicit scene colors:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: x,\n    y: y,\n  },\n\n  theme: {\n    foreground: '#e5e7eb',\n    muted: '#94a3b8',\n    grid: '#334155',\n    background: '#0f172a',\n    palette: ['#38bdf8', '#fb7185', '#4ade80'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`theme` is partial. Omitted fields retain defaults. A responsive definition's\n`chart` context receives the default build-time theme, which is useful when\nmarks need the shared palette or foreground tokens. A `theme` returned by that\nsame builder is merged afterward while the scene is created, so read an\napplication-supplied theme from the builder's captured values when it must use\nthose overrides.\n\nDo not encode semantic status by reading the current theme in data\npreparation. Keep meaning stable and choose theme-appropriate paint at render\ntime.\n\n## Mark styling\n\nBuilt-in marks expose the paint styles relevant to their geometry: fill, stroke,\nopacity, widths, line caps, dashes, corner radius, and font properties. A style\ncan be fixed or data-driven where the mark's option accepts a visual channel.\n\nKeep these responsibilities separate:\n\n- scales map semantic values to visual values;\n- mark options select and refine paint;\n- the theme supplies shared defaults;\n- application CSS controls the surrounding surface.\n\nFor categorical or quantitative color mapping, use the canonical\n[Legends and Color](./legends-and-color.md) guide.\n\n## Axis title styling\n\nString axis titles use the chart foreground and built-in title typography.\nUse the object form when one title needs an explicit text color, opacity, size,\nweight, or offset:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale, axis: { label: 'Month' } },\n    y: {\n      scale: yScale,\n      axis: {\n        label: {\n          text: 'Revenue (USD)',\n          fontSize: 14,\n          fontWeight: 700,\n          fill: '#2563eb',\n          opacity: 0.85,\n        },\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`fill` is the title's text color. The layout measures the configured font size\nand weight before resolving automatic guide margins, so larger titles do not\nneed matching manual margins.\n\n## Canvas styling\n\nThe Canvas renderer resolves scene paints such as `currentColor` and CSS\ncustom properties against the chart's computed environment. It inherits the\nroot font and repaints after relevant ancestor class, style, `data-theme`,\ncolor-scheme, forced-colors, or viewport changes.\n\nRasterized scene nodes are not DOM descendants. A node's `className` therefore\ncannot be targeted by a CSS selector after paint. Put data-dependent fill,\nstroke, opacity, and font choices in mark options or the chart theme; use\ncontainer CSS for palette variables, inherited color, and typography.\n\n## Gradients and clipping\n\nGradients are opt-in resources. Declare them on the chart:\n\n```ts group=gradient-area env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { areaY, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    areaY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'revenue',\n      fill: 'url(#revenue-fill)',\n    }),\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'revenue',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 2,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2) },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Revenue (USD)' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  gradients: [\n    {\n      id: 'revenue-fill',\n      x1: 0,\n      y1: 1,\n      x2: 0,\n      y2: 0,\n      stops: [\n        { offset: 0, color: '#2563eb', opacity: 0.08 },\n        { offset: 1, color: '#2563eb', opacity: 0.7 },\n      ],\n    },\n  ],\n  clip: true,\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=gradient-area file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { month: 'Jan', revenue: 36_000 },\n  { month: 'Feb', revenue: 48_000 },\n  { month: 'Mar', revenue: 45_000 },\n  { month: 'Apr', revenue: 62_000 },\n  { month: 'May', revenue: 76_000 },\n  { month: 'Jun', revenue: 71_000 },\n]\n```\n\nUse `url(#revenue-fill)` as the mark paint. Default SVG hosts emit and scope the\nresource; `idPrefix` keeps resource and clip IDs distinct when several charts\nshare a document.\n\nSet `clip: true` when marks should be clipped to the resolved plot rectangle.\nClipping is a geometry policy, not a substitute for correct scale domains.\n\nSVG and React Native consume both linear and radial gradients for fills and\nstrokes. Canvas consumes linear fills and strokes plus radial fills. It maps a\nradial fill through each shape's normalized bounds, so a non-square shape has\nthe same `objectBoundingBox` ellipse as SVG, then clips the paint to the shape.\nA Canvas gradient needs measurable node bounds; path-only geometry with no\npoint bounds should use an explicit paint instead. Canvas rejects radial\nstrokes because the required nonuniform transform would distort stroke width.\n\n## HTML tooltip styling\n\nThe built-in DOM tooltip is an HTML element inside the chart container by default.\nGive it a class through `tooltip.className` and style that class in application\nCSS:\n\n```ts\nimport { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\n\nconst definition = defineChart(baseDefinition, {\n  tooltip: { use: tooltip, className: 'revenue-tooltip' },\n})\n```\n\nThe default tooltip chrome also reads CSS variables from the chart container.\nThis avoids selector specificity fights with its positioning styles:\n\n```css\n.revenue-chart {\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-background: color-mix(in srgb, Canvas 92%, transparent);\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-color: CanvasText;\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-border: 1px solid\n    color-mix(in srgb, CanvasText 12%, transparent);\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-border-radius: 0.625rem;\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-shadow: 0 12px 34px\n    color-mix(in srgb, CanvasText 14%, transparent);\n}\n```\n\n`--ts-chart-tooltip-max-width`, `--ts-chart-tooltip-padding`, and\n`--ts-chart-tooltip-font` control the remaining surface defaults. A\n`className` is still useful for content-specific layout.\n\nWith the `portal` extension, the preferred manual-Popover path keeps the\nelement under the chart in the DOM, so inheritance and scoped selectors\ncontinue to work. If Popover is unavailable or fails, the fixed fallback moves\nthe element under the chart's `ownerDocument` body. Use a document-level\nselector for its class and put required fallback tokens on that class or a\nshared document ancestor.\n\nEvery framework adapter can compose native application content with the\ndefault rows. See [Tooltips and Focus](./tooltips-and-focus.md).\n\nSee [Themes and Motion examples](../examples/themes-and-motion.md) for complete\ncards with inherited palettes, gradients, controls, and optional motion.\n\n## Theme checklist\n\n- Default charts inherit text color and respond to the application's color\n  scheme.\n- Palette CSS variables are scoped to the smallest useful container.\n- Semantic status remains distinguishable without color alone.\n- Explicit chart themes meet contrast requirements in every supported mode.\n- Resource IDs use a stable `idPrefix` when charts share a document.\n- A theme change does not recreate application data or lose focused state.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Themes and Styling\ndescription: Apply automatic light and dark color behavior, CSS palette tokens, mark styles, and renderer-aware resources.\n---\n\nTanStack Charts inherits the surrounding application instead of installing a\nglobal visual theme. The default chart theme uses:\n\n- `currentColor` for foreground, muted text, and grids;\n- `transparent` for the chart background;\n- six CSS-variable-backed categorical colors.\n\nSet the container's `color` and the chart follows normal light and dark CSS:\n\n```css\n.chart-card {\n  color: #172033;\n  background: #ffffff;\n}\n\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\n  .chart-card {\n    color: #e6edf7;\n    background: #111827;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Palette tokens\n\nOverride the default categorical palette at any container boundary:\n\n```css\n.revenue-chart {\n  --ts-chart-1: #2563eb;\n  --ts-chart-2: #f97316;\n  --ts-chart-3: #10b981;\n  --ts-chart-4: #8b5cf6;\n  --ts-chart-5: #ec4899;\n  --ts-chart-6: #06b6d4;\n}\n```\n\nThis is the lowest-cost path for application branding. It also preserves\nautomatic theme changes without rebuilding a chart definition.\n\n## Definition-level theme\n\nUse `theme` when a chart needs explicit scene colors:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: x,\n    y: y,\n  },\n\n  theme: {\n    foreground: '#e5e7eb',\n    muted: '#94a3b8',\n    grid: '#334155',\n    background: '#0f172a',\n    palette: ['#38bdf8', '#fb7185', '#4ade80'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`theme` is partial. Omitted fields retain defaults. A responsive definition's\n`chart` context receives the default build-time theme, which is useful when\nmarks need the shared palette or foreground tokens. A `theme` returned by that\nsame builder is merged afterward while the scene is created, so read an\napplication-supplied theme from the builder's captured values when it must use\nthose overrides.\n\nDo not encode semantic status by reading the current theme in data\npreparation. Keep meaning stable and choose theme-appropriate paint at render\ntime.\n\n## Mark styling\n\nBuilt-in marks expose the paint styles relevant to their geometry: fill, stroke,\nopacity, widths, line caps, dashes, corner radius, and font properties. A style\ncan be fixed or data-driven where the mark's option accepts a visual channel.\n\nKeep these responsibilities separate:\n\n- scales map semantic values to visual values;\n- mark options select and refine paint;\n- the theme supplies shared defaults;\n- application CSS controls the surrounding surface.\n\nFor categorical or quantitative color mapping, use the canonical\n[Legends and Color](./legends-and-color.md) guide.\n\n## Axis title styling\n\nString axis titles use the chart foreground and built-in title typography.\nUse the object form when one title needs an explicit text color, opacity, size,\nweight, or offset:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale, axis: { label: 'Month' } },\n    y: {\n      scale: yScale,\n      axis: {\n        label: {\n          text: 'Revenue (USD)',\n          fontSize: 14,\n          fontWeight: 700,\n          fill: '#2563eb',\n          opacity: 0.85,\n        },\n      },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`fill` is the title's text color. The layout measures the configured font size\nand weight before resolving automatic guide margins, so larger titles do not\nneed matching manual margins.\n\n## Canvas styling\n\nThe Canvas renderer resolves scene paints such as `currentColor` and CSS\ncustom properties against the chart's computed environment. It inherits the\nroot font and repaints after relevant ancestor class, style, `data-theme`,\ncolor-scheme, forced-colors, or viewport changes.\n\nRasterized scene nodes are not DOM descendants. A node's `className` therefore\ncannot be targeted by a CSS selector after paint. Put data-dependent fill,\nstroke, opacity, and font choices in mark options or the chart theme; use\ncontainer CSS for palette variables, inherited color, and typography.\n\n## Gradients and clipping\n\nGradients are opt-in resources. Declare them on the chart:\n\n```ts group=gradient-area env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { areaY, defineChart, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    areaY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'revenue',\n      fill: 'url(#revenue-fill)',\n    }),\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'revenue',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 2,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: () => scalePoint<string>().padding(0.2) },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Revenue (USD)' },\n    },\n  },\n\n  gradients: [\n    {\n      id: 'revenue-fill',\n      x1: 0,\n      y1: 1,\n      x2: 0,\n      y2: 0,\n      stops: [\n        { offset: 0, color: '#2563eb', opacity: 0.08 },\n        { offset: 1, color: '#2563eb', opacity: 0.7 },\n      ],\n    },\n  ],\n  clip: true,\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=gradient-area file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport const rows = [\n  { month: 'Jan', revenue: 36_000 },\n  { month: 'Feb', revenue: 48_000 },\n  { month: 'Mar', revenue: 45_000 },\n  { month: 'Apr', revenue: 62_000 },\n  { month: 'May', revenue: 76_000 },\n  { month: 'Jun', revenue: 71_000 },\n]\n```\n\nUse `url(#revenue-fill)` as the mark paint. Default SVG hosts emit and scope the\nresource; `idPrefix` keeps resource and clip IDs distinct when several charts\nshare a document.\n\nSet `clip: true` when marks should be clipped to the resolved plot rectangle.\nClipping is a geometry policy, not a substitute for correct scale domains.\n\nSVG and React Native consume both linear and radial gradients for fills and\nstrokes. Canvas consumes linear fills and strokes plus radial fills. It maps a\nradial fill through each shape's normalized bounds, so a non-square shape has\nthe same `objectBoundingBox` ellipse as SVG, then clips the paint to the shape.\nA Canvas gradient needs measurable node bounds; path-only geometry with no\npoint bounds should use an explicit paint instead. Canvas rejects radial\nstrokes because the required nonuniform transform would distort stroke width.\n\n## HTML tooltip styling\n\nThe built-in DOM tooltip is an HTML element inside the chart container by default.\nGive it a class through `tooltip.className` and style that class in application\nCSS:\n\n```ts\nimport { tooltip } from '@tanstack/charts/tooltip'\n\nconst definition = defineChart(baseDefinition, {\n  tooltip: { use: tooltip, className: 'revenue-tooltip' },\n})\n```\n\nThe default tooltip chrome also reads CSS variables from the chart container.\nThis avoids selector specificity fights with its positioning styles:\n\n```css\n.revenue-chart {\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-background: color-mix(in srgb, Canvas 92%, transparent);\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-color: CanvasText;\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-border: 1px solid\n    color-mix(in srgb, CanvasText 12%, transparent);\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-border-radius: 0.625rem;\n  --ts-chart-tooltip-shadow: 0 12px 34px\n    color-mix(in srgb, CanvasText 14%, transparent);\n}\n```\n\n`--ts-chart-tooltip-max-width`, `--ts-chart-tooltip-padding`, and\n`--ts-chart-tooltip-font` control the remaining surface defaults. A\n`className` is still useful for content-specific layout.\n\nWith the `portal` extension, the preferred manual-Popover path keeps the\nelement under the chart in the DOM, so inheritance and scoped selectors\ncontinue to work. If Popover is unavailable or fails, the fixed fallback moves\nthe element under the chart's `ownerDocument` body. Use a document-level\nselector for its class and put required fallback tokens on that class or a\nshared document ancestor.\n\nEvery framework adapter can compose native application content with the\ndefault rows. See [Tooltips and Focus](./tooltips-and-focus.md).\n\nSee [Themes and Motion examples](../examples/themes-and-motion.md) for complete\ncards with inherited palettes, gradients, controls, and optional motion.\n\n## Theme checklist\n\n- Default charts inherit text color and respond to the application's color\n  scheme.\n- Palette CSS variables are scoped to the smallest useful container.\n- Semantic status remains distinguishable without color alone.\n- Explicit chart themes meet contrast requirements in every supported mode.\n- Resource IDs use a stable `idPrefix` when charts share a document.\n- A theme change does not recreate application data or lose focused state.\n"}
